{"arguments":{"patch":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: {{workspace:task/kaitlyn-nguyen/litellm-retry-after-v2}}/artifacts/reviews/litellm-34399.md\n+# LiteLLM #34399 retry/failover review\n+\n+## Scope\n+\n+Pinned source: `litellm@8f86c87f8e065343af6e74e0823ab8a8276b9528`\n+\n+Issue snapshot: `/code/upstream-issues/litellm-34399.md`\n+\n+Changed code:\n+\n+- `/code/litellm/litellm/router.py`\n+- `/code/litellm/tests/local_testing/test_router_retries.py`\n+\n+## Retry precedence\n+\n+`Router._time_to_sleep_before_retry` now applies this precedence:\n+\n+1. When the upstream response supplies `Retry-After`, calculate and honor that provider backoff even if another deployment is healthy.\n+2. When no `Retry-After` applies and another deployment is healthy, return zero for immediate router failover.\n+3. Otherwise, use LiteLLM's existing retry calculation and configured minimum retry delay.\n+\n+This is deliberately scoped to explicit upstream backoff. It does not infer that every deployment shares quota, and it does not delay failover for rate-limit responses without `Retry-After`.\n+\n+## Regression coverage\n+\n+The mapped retry test now models two deployments with the same model and API base, representing one shared rate-limited upstream. It asserts that a 60-second `Retry-After` survives the healthy-deployment path. A companion assertion verifies that the same multi-deployment topology still fails over immediately when the upstream supplies no backoff.\n+\n+No paid provider calls are used.\n+\n+## Checks\n+\n+- `python3 -m py_compile litellm/router.py tests/local_testing/test_router_retries.py`: passed\n+- Focused pytest selection: not executed because the mounted environment has no `pytest` module (`/usr/bin/python3: No module named pytest`)\n+- `python -m pytest`: not executed because no `python` executable is installed\n+\n+## Operational consequence\n+\n+For deployments that share a provider quota, retries no longer consume the router retry budget inside the provider's stated cooldown window. This reduces retry amplification, queue pressure, and secondary 5xxs. Deployments without an explicit upstream cooldown retain zero-delay failover.\n+\n+## Rollback\n+\n+Revert the `Retry-After` guard in `Router._time_to_sleep_before_retry` so the healthy-deployment check again returns zero before header evaluation, and revert the two mapped regression assertions. No data migration or configuration rollback is required.\n+\n+## Evidence\n+\n+- `/code/upstream-issues/litellm-34399.md` (`enterprise_2034a8245bcfb88af6beb9e8`): pinned issue and acceptance criteria\n+- `/knowledge/company/sources/google_drive/shared_drives/engineering/platform-engineering/sre-incident-review-retry-storm-emergency-quota-policy.json` (`dsid_071db3dff7ca4a8db1f1f0f01bb8853c`): aggressive retries amplified 429/5xx traffic; `Retry-After` was an effective mitigation\n+- `/knowledge/company/sources/jira/customer-support/SUP-201992-client-retry-amplification-and-hidden-quota-throttles.json` (`dsid_205c62f1ace8402abf9820662d82ccef`): retry amplification increased queue depth and 503s; increased backoff reduced observed errors from 12% to 2% in matched bursts\n+- `/knowledge/company/sources/linear/engineering/ENG-5132-support-playbook-sdk-retry-timeout.json` (`dsid_41d3e4179d7c4312baf67b9a6801ac9`): support guidance requires respecting `Retry-After` and controlling concurrency\n*** End Patch\n"},"call_id":"call_VzGMcBiLPcsRO1XPVdq82tsv","expected":{"exit_code":0,"status":"ok"},"kind":"patch","name":"apply_patch","record_type":"event","schema_version":1,"sequence":19,"timestamp":"2026-07-28T02:21:28.726Z"}
